# Runtime/user state. Home Assistant mounts this separately in production.
/floorplan_studio/app/data/

# The optional developer fixture is one real household: 179 entity ids naming
# its rooms, device models, and coordinates. The public suite defaults to the
# invented test/house/ and only adds this private fixture when it is present.
# It is useful locally and not publishable. See README, "Running the suite".
/fixtures/

# Working notes and internal design documents. Kept on disk as context for
# future work (including AI assistants) and deliberately not published: they
# describe unreleased state, and REPLICATION.md compares against a separate
# private system in the parent workspace.
/repo-memory/

# Dependencies and test output
node_modules/
coverage/
.nyc_output/

# Local configuration and secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.local

# Logs, caches, and temporary/editor files
*.log
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.cache/
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# IDE metadata
.claude/
.idea/
.vscode/
*.code-workspace

# Operating-system metadata
.DS_Store
._*
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# Keys and certificates, wholesale.
#
# A private key in a public repository is a private key on the internet, and
# "it was only a throwaway test key" is what everyone says. GitHub's push
# protection scans for these and will reject the push, which is the good
# outcome; the bad one is a pattern nobody notices until a real key lands next
# to a fake one.
#
# The TLS test fixtures live under test/fixtures-tls/ and are excluded by this.
# The suite skips the HTTPS test when they are absent and runs it when they are
# there — see "Running the suite" in README.md for the one openssl command that
# creates them.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_ed25519
